  DIFFERENT degraded engine pass than the one the consensus chose). Per-engine
  vote also showed the engines disagree among themselves on these digits
  (e.g. p275: tess/cons read 183, doctr/surya read 133) -- textbook OCR digit noise.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-06-22] PHASE 4 -- ROOT CAUSE | OK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROOT CAUSE: ordinary per-page OCR digit-substitution noise in the BODY
act-heading ("CHAPTER N"), surfaced by the dup-audit's H5 heuristic which
compared the consensus-resolved chapter_int against a *fresh, un-consensused*
re-read of the heading line. The two reads came from different engine passes;
where the heading digit is a 5/0 or 3/8 the fresh read disagrees, producing the
illusory "+/-5 / +/-50 cohort". The 1913 & 1917 volumes stand out only because
their print/scan quality makes 5<->0 and 3<->8 slightly more frequent -- there is
NO systematic merge/sequence bug. The filed chapter_int spine is sound
(monotonic, dup-free, 1..N) in both volumes.

VERDICT: NOT a systematic per-volume bug. NO deterministic "shift block X..Y by
+50" correction is warranted -- applying one would CORRUPT correct data. The
filed chapter_int values are already right; it is the AUDIT's heading re-read
that is wrong on these rows.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-06-22] PHASE 5 -- PROPOSAL (do NOT apply) | OK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. DATA FIX: none. Do not modify any filed chapter_int for the 1913/1917 H5
   candidates. All high/med rows verified filed-correct. Count to "fix": 0.

2. AUDIT FIX (the real defect is in the dup-audit, not the corpus): the H5
   heuristic should not flag a heading mismatch when:
     (a) the filed chapter_int sits in clean monotonic order between its
         confirmed page-neighbors, AND
     (b) the disagreement is a single-digit substitution on a known OCR-confusable
         pair (5/0, 3/8, 8/3, 5/9, 7/1, dropped digit).
   Adding that guard would clear all 24 (1913) + 12 (1917) H5 candidates as
   false positives, and would similarly de-noise the other "cohort" years
   (1907/1919/1921/1923 show the identical 5->0 / 3->8 signature in the same log).

3. RESIDUAL RISK: negligible for the data (we verified the spine). The only
   residual is the small set of genuine sequence GAPS (1913: 63, 1917: 30) which
   are a SEPARATE lostheader/missing-chapter question, not an H5 mis-number, and
   are out of scope here.

